# TLS termination for the home app, so the relay PWA's "AI content" tab can
# iframe it (an https page can't embed http — mixed content — and iOS won't
# tolerate a self-signed cert).
#
# The hostname is public (DuckDNS) but resolves to a private LAN IP, and the
# certificate is obtained via DNS-01. Net effect: a browser-trusted padlock on
# the LAN, with zero ports forwarded and nothing reachable from outside.
#
# Domain and upstream come from the environment (see .env, written by
# `./deploy proxy`), so this file holds no secrets and no site-specific names.
#
# There is deliberately NO `ca` directive: Caddy defaults to the production
# Let's Encrypt CA. `./deploy proxy --staging` inserts one pointing at the staging
# CA before shipping this file. Do not reintroduce `ca {$ACME_CA}` — an unset
# variable expands to nothing, leaving a bare `ca` that Caddy refuses to parse,
# and env_file is only read when a container is CREATED, so a Restart won't
# populate it.
#
# admin.html is blocked here on purpose — part of the central-login migration
# (see homelab-auth/README.md). Everything else this domain serves (mobile.html,
# display.html, recipes.html, and the API they share) stays exactly as open as
# it always was; only the admin console moved behind a real login, reachable
# at http://family-calendar.servers.zou/admin.html (LAN-only, via Traefik) —
# leaving it reachable here too would make that gate pointless, since anyone
# on the LAN could just keep using this hostname instead. 404, not a redirect
# to the gated URL: this domain resolves publicly (see above), and a redirect
# would advertise the internal hostname to anything that ever requests this
# path here, including scanners with no reason to know it exists.
{$DUCKDNS_DOMAIN} {
	tls {
		dns duckdns {env.DUCKDNS_TOKEN}
	}
	handle /admin.html {
		respond 404
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy {$BACKEND_ADDR}
	}
}
